course: dictionary
lesson: 11186
title: Wish: want, hope and say
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wish is a want or a hope. When you wish something, you want it to happen, even if it may not. «I wish you a happy birthday» means you hope the birthday is happy. Look at the words around wish to see which meaning it has.
explain: Stories often use wish for magic. «She made a wish, then blew out the candles» means she hoped for something special, and the candles and the birthday cake are the clues. In a fairy tale, a wish is a request that magic makes come true.
explain: Wish can also be a noun, a thing you hope for. «Best wishes» are kind words at the end of a letter or card. «Alice wishes her cat Dinah were there» uses wish as a verb, because Alice is doing the wanting. Ask yourself: is the word doing something, or is it naming a thing?
fact: Wish can be a verb: I wish, she wishes, we wished, they are wishing.
fact: Wish can be a noun: a wish, two wishes, best wishes.
fact: To wish someone something means to say you hope they have it, as in «I wish you well».
fact: «Wish» means to want something to happen, even if it may not.
fact: Josh's sentence «I was hopping for a sunny day» uses hopping to mean hoping, and the sunny day clue shows it is not about jumping.
exercise: «I wish you a happy birthday.» In this sentence, what does wish mean? | To ask a magic genie for a thing | To take away a present | To jump up and down | *To say you hope someone has something good
exercise: «Feeling lonely, Alice wishes her cat Dinah were there.» What does wishes show? | Alice is making a cake | Alice is fast asleep | Dinah is already there, looking at Alice | *Alice wants Dinah to be there, even though she is not
exercise: Josh writes «I was hopping for a sunny day». What does he mean? | *Hoping, which means wishing for a sunny day | Making a list of sunny days | Eating ice cream in the sun | Jumping on one leg all day
test: «Best wishes» are found where? | *At the end of a letter or card | Inside a sock | On the top of a mountain | At the start of a maths sum
test: «The wish to fool someone is what makes it a lie.» What makes it a lie? | *The wish to fool someone | The length of the sentence | The paper it is written on | The colour of the words
exam: In «She made a wish, then blew out the candles», is wish a noun or a verb? | Adjective | Verb | *Noun
